<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transitional Federal Government</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2004">2004</a>–<a href="/years/2012">2012</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Transitional Federal Government was the internationally recognised government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> from 2004 to 2012. Formed at peace talks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, with a parliament chosen by clan quotas, it struggled to control even Mogadishu and depended on Ethiopian troops and then on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> force <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union-mission-to-somalia">AMISOM</a>. In 2012 it was replaced by the Federal Government of Somalia under a new provisional constitution.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The government of the Somali Democratic Republic collapsed in early 1991, and between 1991 and 2000 no central government existed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transitional-national-government">Transitional National Government</a> formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a> in 2000 was opposed from the start by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, which feared that an independent Somali state would renew its claims on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a> region and sponsored opposition alliances against it. Its successor, set up in Nairobi under the Transitional Federal Charter, was the 14th attempt to establish a central government since the civil war began.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In October 2004 Abdullahi Yusuf Ahmed was elected president with strong Ethiopian backing, and he appointed Ali Mohammed Gedi as prime minister. In March 2005 a parliamentary vote rejecting the deployment of foreign troops, Ethiopian forces among them, ended in a brawl and was declared invalid. The government moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> in 2005 and in February 2006 made Baidoa its seat after a deal with the warlord who held the city. It was installed in Mogadishu after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s 2006 invasion overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-courts-union">Islamic Courts Union</a>, which set off an insurgency led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-shabaab">Al-Shabaab</a>.</p><p>By 2008 most of the country was under insurgent control and more than 80% of the police and army had deserted. Talks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a> between 31 May and 9 June 2008, brokered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> envoy Ahmedou Ould-Abdallah, produced an agreement with the Alliance for the Re-liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> for the withdrawal of Ethiopian troops. Yusuf resigned when parliament began impeachment proceedings, parliament was enlarged to 550 seats, and the former Islamic Courts chairman Sharif Sheikh Ahmed was elected president. With the help of the African Union Mission in Somalia the government took control of Mogadishu in 2011, and its mandate ended on 20 August 2012.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Transitional Federal Parliament elected the president and prime minister and could pass and veto laws. Each of the four major clans held 61 seats and an alliance of minority clans 31; after the alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-courts-union">Islamic Courts Union</a> and other Islamist groups these were given 200 seats, while citizens' groups and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> diaspora held 75. At least 12% of members had to be women. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-european-union">Council of Ministers</a> began with 42 offices and was cut to 18 posts in 2010. Clan-based militias dominated the security forces, and human rights organisations documented serious violations of humanitarian law, widespread corruption and the looting and blockading of aid.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Transitional Federal Government of Somalia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Transitional National Government formed in 2000 and a <b>Transitional Federal Government</b> in 2004.</blockquote><a href="/state/somalia">Somalia</a></li></ul></section>
